he search phase\",\n    \"influence-guided removal is far tighter than random removal, and permuted-outcome controls need larger budgets than real data\",\n    \"three search variants tied on the search-stage score, so the exact-refit-verified greedy is already near the achievable budget on the large crowd table\",\n    \"flip budget depends on the score gap relative to noise and on the evaluation design\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model paired comparisons maximum likelihood estimation ranking\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Defines the scoring model refit exactly in every flip-budget verification; needs the original model paper and standard MLE/fitting references.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Closest methodological antecedent: first-order influence to find a small data subset whose removal changes a conclusion, which the greedy search re-linearises and verifies exactly.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"understanding black-box predictions influence functions\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source of the influence-function machinery underlying the greedy removal ranking; reviewers will expect it as the first-order comparator.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena open platform evaluating LLMs human preference\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Describes the crowd-vote data (55k/140k battles) and the Bradley-Terry ranking pipeline being stress-tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"judging LLM-as-a-judge MT-Bench human expert pairwise judgments\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Provides the expert-judgment and GPT-4 judge comparison data, the small contrasting evaluation design with only a few models.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"improving model ranking Chatbot Arena vote rigging\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Adversarial manipulation of crowd votes is the deliberate-perturbation counterpart to the minimal-removal flip budgets reported.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"leaderboard illusion Chatbot Arena evaluation practices sampling private testing\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Documents data-access and sampling asymmetries that affect the same leaderboard, relevant for interpreting small flip budgets as fragility.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"adversarial manipulation robustness rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Comparator literature on how few corrupted comparisons can change rankings from pairwise data, framing the removal-search results beyond LLM leaderboards.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Elo rating system relation to Bradley-Terry sports game outcomes\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Connects the NBA Elo game-record data (sports evaluation design) to Bradley-Terry-type fitting, needed to justify cross-design comparison.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"statistical inference uncertainty ranking from pairwise comparisons confidence intervals bootstrap\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Flip budgets are interpreted against sampling noise (gap-to-noise); needs the ranking-uncertainty literature that leaderboard confidence intervals rely on.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"generic optimization or benchmarking surveys\",\n    \"scientific method or paper-writing phrasing\",\n    \"chronology of cycles, attempts or search history\",\n    \"internal score names such as combined_score or rubric\",\n    \"titles, authors or results of the withheld published paper on this research question, or pages citing it\",\n    \"unrelated LLM architecture or training queries\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\nEOF\ncat > reference_query_plan.md <<'EOF'\n# Reference Query Plan\n\n**Central topic.** How few comparisons must be removed from a pairwise-comparison dataset to flip a Bradley-Terry leaderboard ranking (verified by exact refit), and how this differs across crowd-vote (Chatbot Arena), expert/LLM-judge (MT-Bench) and sports (NBA Elo) data.\n\n**Grounded evidence.** Three search variants (row-level influence greedy; cell-aggregated relaxed search with swap polish; stationarity-LP over target ratings) tied on the search-stage score. Crowd-vote instances flipped with tiny budgets and the small expert table was not flipped within the cap. Influence-guided removal beat random removal, and permuted-outcome controls needed larger budgets. The final evaluation reported flip budgets on 52-model (135,630 rows) and 30-model (12,900 rows) tables.\n\n## Queries (10)\n1. Bradley-Terry model paired comparisons maximum likelihood estimation ranking (core_method): the model being refit.\n2. approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness (core_method): nearest methodological antecedent.\n3. understanding black-box predictions influence functions (baseline_comparator): first-order influence machinery.\n4. Chatbot Arena open platform evaluating LLMs human preference (benchmark_context): crowd-vote data and pipeline.\n5. judging LLM-as-a-judge MT-Bench human expert pairwise judgments (benchmark_context): expert/judge data.\n6. improving model ranking Chatbot Arena vote rigging (result_driven_theme): deliberate perturbation counterpart.\n7. leaderboard illusion Chatbot Arena evaluation practices sampling private testing (result_driven_theme): leaderboard fragility context.\n8. adversarial manipulation robustness rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons (baseline_comparator): general rank-aggregation robustness.\n9. Elo rating system relation to Bradley-Terry sports game outcomes (benchmark_context): NBA design.\n10. statistical inference uncertainty ranking from pairwise comparisons confidence intervals bootstrap (result_driven_theme): interpreting flip budgets against sampling noise.\n\n## Rejected alternatives\nGeneric \"benchmarking\", \"optimization\" or \"robustness of ML\" queries, chronology-shaped queries about cycles, internal score names, and any query aimed at the withheld published paper on this question. The queries use method and dataset names that the run exercises. No query targets LP or convex-relaxation theory beyond what the Bradley-Terry and influence literatures already cover.\n\n## Forbidden patterns\nGeneric surveys; process/paper-writing phrasing; search-history chronology; internal metric names; the withheld paper's title/authors/results or pages citing it; unrelated LLM architecture queries.\nEOF\ntouch memory.md global_memory/memory.md global_memory/memory.jsonl\nN=\"Reference query plan (2026-11-11): wrote 10 queries on Bradley-Terry, AMIP/influence-based data-dropping robustness, Arena/MT-Bench/NBA-Elo data context, vote rigging, leaderboard illusion, rank-aggregation robustness, ranking uncertainty. Chosen over generic benchmarking/optimization queries because they match methods and datasets actually run; no query targets the withheld paper.\"\nprintf '\\n- %s\\n' \"$N\" >> memory.md; printf '\\n- %s\\n' \"$N\" >> global_memory/memory.md\npython3 - <<EOF\nimport json\nopen('global_memory/memory.jsonl','a').write(json.dumps({\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-11T00:00:00Z\",\"step\":\"reference_plan\",\"scope\":\"paper\",\"summary\":\"$N\"})+\"\\n\")\nEOF\npython3 -c \"import json;print(len(json.load(open('reference_query_plan.json'))['queries']))\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1016,"cache_read_input_tokens":32087,"cache_creation":{